   "summary": "Unlock these with Subscription A 55-year-old man was killed after a luxury car, allegedly driven by a 17-year-old boy, rammed the scooter he was riding. The Cheranalloor police have taken into custody the minor driver, reportedly the son of a senior judge. The accident comes days after the Nedumbassery accident in which three youths were killed after an allegedly over-speeding luxury car driven by a 19-year-old youth rammed a two-wheeler from behind.",
   "summary_zh": "订阅A即可解锁这些内容。一辆据称由17岁少年驾驶的豪华汽车撞上男子乘坐的摩托车，导致一名55岁男子死亡。切拉纳卢尔警方已将这名未成年驾驶者拘捕，据称他是一名高级法官的儿子。这起事故发生数日前，内杜马瑟里发生了一起事故，一辆据称由19岁青年驾驶、超速行驶的豪华汽车从后方撞上一辆两轮车，造成3名青年死亡。",
   "summarySource": "fulltext",
   "body": "Account subscription benefits alongside Premium Stories, Editorials, Opinions and more. Unlock these with Subscription A 55-year-old man was killed after a luxury car, allegedly driven by a 17-year-old boy, rammed the scooter he was riding. The deceased has been identified as Jayabalakrishnan of Chittur, Cheranalloor. The Cheranalloor police have taken into custody the minor driver, reportedly the son of a senior judge. The car is registered in the name of Reno Francis Xavier, First Additional District Judge, of the Irinjalakuda Magistrate Court. The accident comes days after the Nedumbassery accident in which three youths were killed after an allegedly over-speeding luxury car driven by a 19-year-old youth rammed a two-wheeler from behind. The accident occurred around 2.20 a.m. near a car showroom at Maraparambu on Container Road in Cheranalloor. The teenager, who lives in Aluva, was reportedly was travelling alone from Vallarpadam towards Kalamassery when it hit the scooter, which was moving in the same direction. Jayabalakrishnan reportedly succumbed to his injuries on the spot. His relatives told media that he was running an eatery at Chittur. The police have registered a case against the driver on charges of rash driving and rash or negligent act that does not amount to culpable homicide. Comments have to be in English, and in full sentences. They cannot be abusive or personal. Please abide by our community guidelines for posting your comments. We have migrated to a new commenting platform. If you are already a registered user of The Hindu and logged in, you may continue to engage with our articles. If you do not have an account please register and login to post comments. Users can access their older comments by logging into their accounts on Vuukle",
