   "summary": "Barack Obama campaigned for Democratic candidates, framing the upcoming midterm elections as a chance to rein in Trump's presidency and calling for accountability in government. Obama criticized Trump for corruption and ineptitude, highlighting public concerns about the President's financial dealings and foreign policy, particularly regarding Iran. Obama, president from 2009 until Trump began his first term in 2017, remains one of the most popular figures among Democrats.",
   "summary_zh": "贝拉克·奥巴马为民主党候选人开展竞选活动，将即将到来的中期选举定位为遏制特朗普总统任期的机会，并呼吁追究政府责任。奥巴马批评特朗普腐败且无能，强调公众对总统财务交易和外交政策的担忧，尤其是伊朗问题。奥巴马从2009年起担任总统，直至特朗普于2017年开始首个任期；他仍然是民主党人中最受欢迎的人物之一。",
   "summarySource": "fulltext",
   "body": "Already have Rappler+? Sign in to listen to groundbreaking journalism. OBAMA. Former US President Barack Obama campaigns for Democratic US Senate nominee Abdul El-Sayed and Democratic gubernatorial candidate Jocelyn Benson at a rally in Detroit, Michigan, US October 24, 2026. Barack Obama campaigned for Democratic candidates, framing the upcoming midterm elections as a chance to rein in Trump's presidency and calling for accountability in government. Obama criticized Trump for corruption and ineptitude, highlighting public concerns about the President's financial dealings and foreign policy, particularly regarding Iran. The former president's appearances aimed to energize Democratic voters in tight races, using humor and sharp critiques to draw contrasts with the Trump administration. This is AI-generated. Read the article for full context. Report any errors . US President Donald Trump has been telling voters to view the midterm elections as if he were on the ballot, and on Saturday, October 14, one of his Democratic predecessors did just that. Ten days out from the elections that will determine control of the closely divided Congress, Barack Obama took to the campaign trail to accuse the Republican president of corruption, deride his strategy for Iran , and skewer the gilded aesthetic he has applied to White House renovations. Obama stumped for Democratic candidates at rallies in Cleveland and Detroit, lampooning the Trump administration. Campaigning in Michigan for US Senate candidate Abdul El-Sayed, Obama framed the November 3 vote as a “circuit breaker” to “at least rein in some of the foolishness” of Trump’s presidency. “We got to flip that circuit breaker, bring some accountability to this government,” Obama said. Obama, president from 2009 until Trump began his first term in 2017, remains one of the most popular figures among Democrats. They hope that bringing him into the race can fire up their supporters for a tight US Senate race in which El-Sayed faces Republican Mike Rogers, a former US representative. The former president obliged the Michigan crowd by playing to Democrats’ frequently repeated criticisms of Trump and of Republicans’ willingness to go along with him. “We have never seen a president excel in straight-up grifting, on your dime, like this,” Obama said. “Never seen a president so intent on selling favors like this…. Now the party seems to stand for corrupt government, or inept government, or no government.” The White House did not immediately respond to a request for comment. Obama also ridiculed the administration’s claims of victory against Iran. He argued that the war has given Iran more economic leverage because its threats to attack shipping in the Strait of Hormuz have unsettled oil exporters. “Iran now has veto power over the Strait of Hormuz, where oil goes through to get to the world,” Obama said. The former president also accused the Trump administration of emphasizing unimportant matters, sarcastically calling Defense Secretary Pete Hegseth someone “laser-focused on important matters like the jawline and testosterone levels of our troops” and ridiculing White House renovations as tacky. “He’s got the White House looking like Saddam Hussein opened a Cheesecake Factory,” Obama said. – Rappler.com Upgrade to Rappler+ for exclusive content and unlimited access",
